Why Git History is Dangerous ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Imagine this:

Commit 1
password=Admin123

↓

Commit 2
Developer deletes password

↓

Commit 3
Current code looks clean

Question:

Is the password really gone?

❌ No.

Git keeps the complete history.

Someone can simply run:

git log
git show
git checkout <old_commit>

and recover the secret.

This is why companies treat accidentally committed secrets as compromised, even if they are removed later.

The correct action is usually:

Rotate the password/token.
Create a new credential.
Revoke the old one.
Enterprise Security Principle #1

Assume any secret committed to Git is compromised.

Not "might be."

Assume it is.

